          %p
            A
            %em spectral triple
            (
            %a{:href => "#ConnesMoscovici95"}> Connes-Moscovici 95
            ) is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebraic
            data that mimics the geometric data provided by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            (
            %a{:href => "#Connes08"}> Connes 08
            ) and generalizes it to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+module"} Fredholm module
            with possibly unbounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
            and refined by the specification of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subalgebra"} dense subalgebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} C-star-algebra
            of bounded operators on that module. As such, spectral triples have close ties to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            and so also to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            described by these (see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
          %p In a little more detail, a spectral triple consists of
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-graded" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℋ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subalgebra"} dense embedding
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow B(H)
                into the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} C-star-algebra
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
                on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                These two items encode the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                acting on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
                and satisfying some conditions, to be thought of as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                acting on the spinors.
              %p
                This item encodes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
                and possibly a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
          %p
            (This is, or is a slight variant of, the concept of an unbounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+modules"} Fredholm modules
            (e.g.
            = succeed "))" do
              %a{:href => "#CareyPhilips98"} Carey-Philips 98
          %p
            Below we discuss how one may think of a spectral triple as being precisely the algebraic data of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            defining the worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            of the quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} super particle
            propagating on a Riemannian target space (a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
            is algebraic data encoding a Riemannian manifold with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
          %h3#As1DimensionalFQFTs As 1-dimensional FQFTs
          %p
            Here is an
            %strong unorthodox way
            to state the idea of spectral triple in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            motivation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            derived his definition from in the modern light of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"} Soibelman
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Soibelman11"} Soibelman 11
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
          %blockquote
            %p (but maybe eventually we should have a traditional idea section and move this here to a subsection on further interpretations)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 1
                    %mi Feyn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Cob_{1|1}^{Feyn}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graph"} Feynman graph
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            with a single type of interaction along the lines of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1|1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\bullet
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp; \to
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \\bullet
                  }
          %p subject to the obvious associativity condition.
          %p
            Then a spectral triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,H,D)
            is the data encoding a sufficiently nice smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi H
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 1
                    %mi Feyn
                  %mo →
                  %mi sVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_{(A,H,D)} : R Cob_{1|1}^{Feyn}  \to sVect
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo •
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = Z_{(A,H,D)}(\bullet)_0
                is the even part of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
                assigned by the functor to the point, equipped with the structure of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                whose product is given by the image of the interaction vertex
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↘
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↗
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Z_{(A,H,D)}
                      \\left(
                      \\array{
                      \\bullet
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \searrow
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp; \to
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \nearrow
                      \\\
                      \\bullet
                      }
                      \\right)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                is some completion of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_{(A,H,D)}(\bullet)
                to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Hilbert+space"} super Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi End
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in End(H)
                is an odd self-adjoint operator on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,\theta)
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi t
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mi θ
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (t,\theta) \mapsto \exp( - t D^2 + \theta D )
                      \\,.
          %p (For technical details that I am glossing over see the field theory link above).
          %p
            So this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "in" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = L^2(S)
                is the space of square integrable spinor sections;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = C^\infty(X)
                is the space of smooth functions on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            One point of a spectral triple is to take the view of world-line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            as basic and
            %em characterize
            the spin Riemannian geometry of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            entirely by this algebraic data. In particular the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+spectrum"} operator spectrum
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Then with all the ordinary geoemtry re-encoded algebraically this way, in terms of the 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that
            %em probes
            this geometry, one can then use the same formulas to interpret spectral triple geometrically that do
            %em not
            come from an ordinary geometry as in the above example.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#metric_dimension_and_kodimension Metric dimension and KO-dimension
          %p
            For a space described by a spectral triple there are several notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            which all coincide when the space is a classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            but which may differ on more general spectral spaces.
          %p
            Notably there is the
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                metric dimension
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/metric+dimension"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalues"} eigenvalues
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            (
            %a{:href => "#Connes95"}> Connes95, p. 6
            ).
          %p
            And then there is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+geometry"} spectral geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hearing+the+shape+of+a+drum"} hearing the shape of a drum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"}> higher category theory and physics
                \:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "higher+category+theory+and+physics#SpecStandModAndGravity"} Spectral standard model and gravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The standard textbook is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              %em Noncommutative Geometry
              , Academic Press (1994)
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masoud+Khalkhali"} Masoud Khalkhali
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Basic Noncommutative Geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://ems.press/books/elm/225"} ems:elm/225
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.nagoya-u.ac.jp/~richard/teaching/s2015/Khalkhali_13.pdf"} pdf
          %p The terminology of spectral triples was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#ConnesMoscovici95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Moscovici"} Henri Moscovici
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Local Index Formula in Noncommutative Geometry
            %li#Connes95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Noncommutative geometry and reality
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.531241"} doi:10.1063/1.531241
          %p
            and that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              %em Gravity coupled with matter and foundation of non-commutative geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9603053"}> arXiv:hep-th/9603053
              )
          %p
            The characterization of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            in terms of spectral triples is in
          %ul
            %li#Connes08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              %em On the spectral characterization of manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.2088"}> arXiv:0810.2088
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#CareyPhilips98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Carey"}> Alan Carey
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Phillips"} John Phillips
                %em Fredholm modules and spectral flow
                J. Canadian Math. Soc.
                %strong 50
                (1998) 673-718 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://cms.math.ca/10.4153/CJM-1998-038-x"}> cms:10.4153/CJM-1998-038-x
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
                  and their
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+flow"} spectral flow
            %li
              %p
                R. da Rocha, A. A. Tomaz,
                %em Hearing the shape of inequivalent spin structures and exotic Dirac operators
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03619"}> arXiv:2003.03619
                )
          %p
            Traditionally spectral triples are discussed without specifying their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of spectral triples include the following
          %ul
            %li#BCL05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Bertozzini"}> Paolo Bertozzini
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Conti"} Roberto Conti
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Category of Spectral Triples and Discrete Groups with Length Function
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0502583"} arXiv:math/0502583
            %li#BCL08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Bertozzini"}> Paolo Bertozzini
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Conti"} Roberto Conti
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Non-Commutative Geometry, Categories and Quantum Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0801.2826"} arXiv:0801.2826
          %p
            (See also the pointers concerning the relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesRelationToKKTheory"} below
          %p#OverJordanAlgebras
            Spectral triples over
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebras"} Jordan algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Latham+Boyle"}> Latham Boyle
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shane+Farnsworth"} Shane Farnsworth
                %em The standard model, the Pati-Salam model, and “Jordan geometry”
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11888"}> arxiv:1910.11888
                )
            %li
              %p
                Fabien Besnard,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shane+Farnsworth"} Shane Farnsworth
                %em Particle models from special Jordan backgrounds and spectral triples
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07039"} arXiv:2206.07039
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Ludwik Dabrowski, Andrzej Sitarz,
              %em Multiwisted real spectral triples
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.12873"}> arXiv:1911.12873
              )
          %h3#relation_to_quantum_physics Relation to quantum physics
          %p
            A discussion of spectral triples as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            data encoding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of a category of 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian
            structure and vertices is in section 1.4 of
          %ul
            %li#Soibelman11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"}> Yan Soibelman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Collapsing Conformal Field Theories, spaces with non-negative Ricci curvature and non-commutative geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              (eds.)
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
          %p A brief indication of some of the central ideas going into this is at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/06/had_the_pleasure_of_talking.html"} Spectral triples and graph field theory
          %p
            A general introduction to and discussion of spectral triples with an eye on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrg+Fr%C3%B6hlich"}> Jürg Fröhlich
              , Oiver Grandjean,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Recknagel"} Andreas Recknagel
              %em Supersymmetric quantum theory, non-commutative geometry, and gravitation
              Lecture Notes Les Houches (1995) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9706132"}> arXiv:hep-th/9706132
              )
          %p
            In section 7.2 of this an outlokk on how to regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string's
            worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
            is given.
          %p
            A detailed derivation for how spectral triples arise as point particle limits of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Roggenkamp"}> Daniel Roggenkamp
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Wendland"} Katrin Wendland
              %em Limits and Degenerations of Unitary Conformal Field Theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0308143"}> arXiv:hep-th/0308143
              )
          %p A summary of this is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Roggenkamp"}> Daniel Roggenkamp
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Wendland"} Katrin Wendland
              %em Decoding the geometry of conformal field theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0803.0657"}> arXiv:0803.0657
              )
          %p Also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastiano+Carpi"}> Sebastiano Carpi
              , Robin Hillier,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yasuyuki+Kawahigashi"} Yasuyuki Kawahigashi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Longo"} Roberto Longo
              %em Spectral Triples and the Super-Virasoro Algebra
              Communications in Mathematical Physics, Volume 295, Number 1 / April 2010 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.springerlink.com/content/316wx4r4x885172w/fulltext.pdf"}> Springer pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+models"} matrix models
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              T. Asakawa, S. Sugimoto, S. Terashima,
              = succeed "," do
                %em D-branes, Matrix Theory and K-homology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0108085"} arXiv:hep-th/0108085
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
            via finite-dimensional spectral triples:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+W.+Barrett"}> John W. Barrett
              \:
              %em Fermion integrals for finite spectral triples
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.18428"}> arXiv:2403.18428
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#von_neumann_spectral_triples von Neumann spectral triples
          %p
            One variation uses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebras"} C-star algebras
          %p
            This goes back to (
            %a{:href => "#CareyPhilips98"}> Carey-Philips 98
            ) and
          %ul
            %li
              Alan L Carey, John Phillips, Fyodor Sukochev,
              %em
                On unbounded
                = succeed "-summable" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9908091"}> arXiv:math/9908091
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                M-T. Benameur, T. Fack,,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Type II non-commutative geometry. I. Dixmier trace in von Neumann algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Moscovici"} Henri Moscovici
                %em Type III and spectral triples
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0609703"}> arXiv:math/0609703
                )
          %h3#ReferencesRelationToKKTheory Relation to K-theory
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.sc.chula.ac.th/cimpa/sites/default/file/bangkoknotes.pdf&#8206;"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bram+Mesland"}> Bram Mesland
                ,
                %em Spectral triples and KK-theory: A survey
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.3802"}> arXiv:1304.3802
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Carey"}> Alan Carey
                , John Philips, Adam Rennie,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Spectral triples: examples and index theory
                %em Noncommutative geometry and physics, Renormalization, Motives, Index theory
                (2011)
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+2-sphere"} fuzzy 2-sphere
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francesco+D%27Andrea"}> Francesco D'Andrea
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedele+Lizzi"} Fedele Lizzi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+V%C3%A1rilly"} Joseph Várilly
              = succeed "," do
                %em Metric Properties of the Fuzzy Sphere
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.0108"} arXiv:1209.0108
        .revisedby
          %p
